Thanks so much for following up with all this detail.  That's
exactly how we go about trying to diagnose sampling problems like
this.

Another problem you run into is that if you have K degrees of
freedom going into beta0 and run softmax(beta0), then you have
identifiability problems.  You can pin one component of beta0 to
0 for identifiability, but that's awkward because now beta0 is
asymmetric.

If you declare a variable as

  real<lower=0, upper=1> beta;

then beta gets a uniform distribution on (0, 1), which is equivalent to
writing:

  real beta_raw;

  beta_raw ~ logistic(0, 1);

  beta = inv_logit(beta_raw);

In fact, if you work through the Jacobian of the inverse of the logit
transform going on under the hood you get the logistic (inv_logit is
cdf of the unit logitistc and its derivative is the logistic(0, 1) density).

- Bob

