Since foo_raw is always ~normal(0,1) is it necessary to have T versions of it?  Could I do something like:

parameters {
       real foo_raw;
       real sigma;
}

      real foo[N]//Is foo a transformed parameter?

    //Does this go in the model of transformed parameters?
      foo_raw ~ normal(0,1);
      sigma ~ cauchy(0, .5);

      foo[1] = 20 + sigma*foo_raw;

      foo[2:N]=foo[1:N-1] + sigma*foo_raw;

Is foo now a transformed parameter? If so, to constrain the bounds of foo (upper=a, lower=b), must I now constrain foo_raw to constrain foo indirectly?

Yeah this seems to generally be an issue with softmax regression. It's not identified, and there's no base group. It's really about how big each one is relative to all others.

I finally finished out the softmax model (with predictors) and it worked reasonably well. Constraining the beta0s to be positive-logistic distributed, and identifying latent classes with mean ordering seemed to work.
The only "problem" is that even with priors on the intercepts, it can have large or small predicted logits --- However, this matters exactly none, because once you convert to probabilities, they are all equivalent.
You're right though --- If I constrained one group to have a certain logit, then the rest of them will be scaled appropriately.
For the purposes of a multinomial logistic model predicting latent memberships though, the assigned cluster probabilities will remain the same. A bit of label switching here and there, but marginally it seems to work.

I generated a mixture of gaussians such that N1=25,N2=75,N3=50; mean_1 = 0, mean_2 = 7, mean_3 = 4; I don't recall the sd's. However, the model recovered the means and individuals' cluster probabilities strikingly well.

