> Hi Bob,
>
> first of all, please apologise the somehow very bulky piece of code I provided in a first instance and thank you very much for your suggestions with the vectorization. I have tried to tidy up the unnecessary parts (transformation of the data and back transformation to obtain z-scores) and to apply the steps of vectorisation you proposed (although for clarity I have transformed beta to beta0 and beta1, I will probably vectorise this when I'm sure the model is right) . This is the code now:
>
> data{
>     int<lower=0> N;                     //number of total observations
>     int<lower=1> J;                      //number of subjects
>     int<lower=1,upper=J> s[N];     // suject ID
>     vector[J] e;                            // group-level interactions (number of electrodes)
>     vector[N] y;                           // outcome variable
>     vector[N] x;                           // predictor(s)
> }
>
> parameters{
>     real beta_mu[2];                                    // group averages for intercept[1] and slope[2]
>     real<lower=0> sigma_y;                         // total error
>     matrix[2,J] e_betaj;
>     vector<lower=0,upper=100>[2] sigma_b;
>     vector[2] gamma;                                  // random part of group-level predictors (electrode count) (?)
> }
>
> transformed parameters{
>     vector[N] y_hat;
>     vector[J] beta0_raw;
>     vector[J] beta1_raw;
>     vector[J] beta0;
>     vector[J] beta1;
>
>     beta0_raw = beta_mu[1] + sigma_b[1] * e_betaj[1]';
>     beta1_raw = 0.1 * beta_mu[2] + sigma_b[2] * e_betaj[2]';         // why multiplying with 0.1 ?!?
>
>     beta0 = beta0_raw + e * gamma[1];
>     beta1 = beta1_raw + e * gamma[2];
>
>     for (i in 1:N)
>         y_hat[i] = beta0[s[i]] + beta1[s[i]] * x[i];
> }
>
> model {
>     //Priors
>     sigma_y ~ cauchy(0, 2.5);
>     sigma_b ~ cauchy(0, 2.5);
>     beta_mu ~ normal(0, 5);
>     gamma ~ normal(0,1);
>     to_vector(e_betaj) ~ normal(0, 1);
>
>     y ~ student_t(4, y_hat, sigma_y);
> }
>
> Unsurprisingly, it runs much faster than before, but the results remain the same.

At least that means you refactored it properly :-)

> Without the group-level predictor ("e") the slope is negative while after including it, the slope is reversed and the fit of the model is a disaster.

> I haven't found too much on varying intercepts and slopes with group-level predictors but my hunch would be that it is not valid to include the "e * gamma"-term when there is one value of "e" per subject and it ranges from 1-5 the way I'm doing it.

By ranges from 1 to 5 do you mean that there are five unrelated values?  If
so, you probably don't want to multiply but instead index, so that e is declared
as an integer and you use:

   gamma[e[j]]

where one of the gamma is set to 0, say gamma[1] = 0, for identifiability.

If it's ordinal, you can sometimes get away with treating it as a scalar.

> And it may not make much sense but I thought I run the model like this and when this works I extend it incorporating multivariate priors according to chapter 6.12 of the rstan manual as you also suggested (Although if the way I'm doing it is nonsense and it makes more sense to do it in one step, it would be of course helpful to know as well). I know these are rather basic questions and I appreciate the time you are spending. Thanks in advance.

You're debugging this the right way starting from simple things that work and
then adding bits until something breaks so you know where the problem is (or at
least that it's a combination of the new bit and whatever was there before).

- Bob

