I'm trying to wrap my head around a toy example for a much harder problem. I'm interested in modelling the probability of detection of some event conditional on the arrival time of that event. As an example I'm using the exponential distribution. Let the arrival time of the i^th event be distributed as exponential with some rate parameter, lambda. If the arrival time is less than 1, the event is detected with probability p_1; and if the arrival time is greater than 1, the event is detect with with probability p_2. "Detection" is probably the wrong word here though. Because I am assuming that if an event is not detected, we know that it occurred, but we don't the arrival time. In other words, given that arrival time is less than 1 we record a zero with probability (1-p_1) and if arrival time is greater than 1 we record a zero with probability (1-p_2).

